# Architecture standard

{% if profile == "minimal" -%}
Keep the minimal profile direct: `main.py` owns the small public behavior. Do not introduce domain,
application, or adapter layers until a concrete feature needs a stable boundary. When multiple
unrelated capabilities begin accumulating, avoid global `services/`, `models/`, `repositories/`,
and `utils/` buckets; consider migrating to modules.
{% elif profile == "application" -%}
Preserve entrypoint/bootstrap → module application → module domain. Domain must not import
application code, configuration, frameworks, or infrastructure. Bootstrap is the composition
root; configuration is read and validated there, then passed inward explicitly. Load settings once
at this outer boundary; do not read process environment throughout domain, application, or adapters.
{% else -%}
Preserve entrypoint/bootstrap → module adapters/application → module domain. Adapters may depend on
application or domain contracts, never the reverse. Bootstrap selects and wires concrete adapters;
keep framework and side-effect details at those outer boundaries. Load and validate configuration
there, then inject it inward; do not scatter process-environment reads through modules.
{% endif %}

Create packages for clear ownership, not as speculative placeholders. Do not add generic buckets
such as `services`, `models`, `repositories`, `utils`, `helpers`, or `data`.

## Boundaries and contracts

Keep HTTP, filesystem, database, queue, SDK, and vendor I/O behind the owning capability's adapter
or outer boundary. Operations that can block need finite bounds when supported; network calls
always need a finite timeout. Retry only plausibly transient failures when the operation is
idempotent or explicitly made safe, with one clear retry owner. Never retry at multiple layers.
Resources such as files, clients, connections, transactions, and streams need explicit ownership
and deterministic cleanup.

Translate vendor failures into a stable inward-facing error when the meaning matters, preserving
the cause (`raise ApplicationFacingError(...) from exc`). Do not wrap exceptions just for
uniformity. Domain errors represent business rules; entrypoints own final user/protocol rendering
and status mapping. Catch only to recover, translate, or add useful context. Never broadly catch
and ignore, mechanically log and rethrow at every layer, or expose vendor internals by accident.
Libraries and modules must not configure logging globally. Report failures at the user-facing
boundary, log only safe operational context, and avoid duplicate failure logs or secrets and raw
sensitive payloads.

{% if profile != "minimal" -%}
Configuration belongs to `config.py` and is composed in `bootstrap.py`: document environment
variable names, validate required settings before composing services, and inject typed settings
inward. Do not provide real secret defaults, commit secrets or machine-specific configuration, or
log credentials. Use the simplest justified loader; no configuration framework is required.

When capability modules call one another, use an explicit public
application-facing contract. Export that contract from the owning capability's
`application/__init__.py`; other modules must not import its adapters, private domain
implementation, or internal helpers. Start with direct synchronous calls. Events need a real
asynchronous, lifecycle, or integration requirement; `shared/` is not a general cross-module
channel.
{% endif %}

Public contracts include documented CLI behavior and options, documented exit behavior, exported
APIs, persisted formats and schemas, HTTP/event contracts, and documented integrations. Before
changing one, evaluate whether it is breaking, what migration or compatibility path is needed, and
the README, tests, and user documentation impact.
